**`StatsScreen.loadStats()` — two sequential queries at the end** (`mobile/src/screens/StatsScreen.tsx:184`)

Monthly completions and focus sessions are fetched *after* the main 6-query batch, serially:

```ts
// After the Promise.all resolves:
const { data: monthCompletions } = await supabase.from('habit_completions')...  // sequential
const { data: focusData } = await supabase.from('monk_sessions')...              // sequential
```

Fix — include them in the initial batch:

```ts
const [userData, habitsData, completionsData, checkInsData, totalHabits, totalMissions,
       monthCompletions, focusData] = await Promise.all([
  // existing 6 queries...
  supabase.from('habit_completions').select('date, habit_id').eq('user_id', user.id).gte('date', monthStart),
  supabase.from('monk_sessions').select('duration_minutes').eq('user_id', user.id).gte('completed_at', weekAgoISO),
]);
```

---

**`QuoteCard` and `TodayCard` defined as inner functions** (`CoachScreen.tsx:566, 579`)

Both are defined *inside* `CoachScreen`, so they're recreated as new function references on every render. Since `messages` state changes on every chat message, these components re-render even when their data (streak, habits, quote) hasn't changed:

```ts
// Current — recreated every CoachScreen render
const QuoteCard = () => { ... };
const TodayCard = () => { ... };
```

Fix: extract to `React.memo` components outside CoachScreen:

```ts
const QuoteCard = React.memo(({ personality, color, dismissed, onDismiss }) => {
  if (dismissed) return null;
  const quote = getDailyQuote(personality);
  return (...);
});

const TodayCard = React.memo(({ streak, habitsToday, dopamineScore, morningDone, eveningDone }) => {
  ...
});
```

---

**`renderMessage` not memoized in FlatList** (`CoachScreen.tsx:618`)

```ts
// Recreated every render → FlatList sees new prop → re-renders all visible cells
const renderMessage = ({ item }: { item: Message }) => (...)
```

Fix:
```ts
const renderMessage = useCallback(({ item }: { item: Message }) => (
  <AnimatedMessageWrapper>
    ...
  </AnimatedMessageWrapper>
), [personality, color, starredIds, handleStarMessage]); // only update when these change
```

---

**Inline array allocation in JSX** (`CoachScreen.tsx:775`)

```ts
// New array created every render inside JSX — disrupts FlatList memoization
{([[ dot1, dotY1 ], [dot2, dotY2], [dot3, dotY3]] as [Animated.Value, Animated.Value][]).map(...)}
```

Fix — move to a `useRef` or constant outside render:

```ts
const dotPairs = useRef<[Animated.Value, Animated.Value][]>([]);
// populate in component body once:
if (!dotPairs.current.length) {
  dotPairs.current = [[dot1, dotY1], [dot2, dotY2], [dot3, dotY3]];
}
```

---

**Streak calendar IIFE inside render** (`StatsScreen.tsx:404`)

The entire calendar is built via an IIFE (`(() => { ... })()`) directly in JSX, running on every render. Extract to `React.memo`:

```ts
const StreakCalendar = React.memo(({ monthData, rankColor }: { monthData: ..., rankColor: string }) => {
  const now = new Date();
  // ... calendar logic ...
  return <View>...</View>;
});
```

---

**`buildCoachContext` called on every message** — see fix above. 60-second TTL is enough since the data (habits, goals, check-ins) is written by the same user moments apart.

**`getQuickActions()` recalculated every render** (`CoachScreen.tsx:539`)

Time-of-day changes only every 6 hours at most. Memoize:

```ts
const quickActions = useMemo(() => getQuickActions(), []);
// No deps needed — re-mount or focus event is the right refresh boundary
```

**Rate limit query duplicated client + server** (`CoachScreen.tsx:300`, `limits.ts:24`)

The client queries `chat_messages` count to show `messagesLeft`, and the server *also* queries `users.daily_messages` on every message. The server already tracks `daily_messages` atomically — use that as source of truth and return it in the response to skip the client-side query:

```ts
// In limits.ts, return remaining count in the response
return new Response(JSON.stringify({ remaining: FREE_DAILY_LIMIT - user.daily_messages }), ...);

// In invoke(), surface it
if ((data as any)?.remaining !== undefined) {
  // update messagesLeft state from server response, no client-side DB query needed
}
```

---

**`searchDebounce` not cleared on unmount** (`CoachScreen.tsx:213`)

```ts
const searchDebounce = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);

// Current: only cleared in exitSearch(), not on unmount
```

Fix — add cleanup:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  return () => {
    if (searchDebounce.current) clearTimeout(searchDebounce.current);
  };
}, []);
```

---

**Recording animation loop not stopped on unmount** (`CoachScreen.tsx:490`)

`Animated.loop(...).start()` in `startRecording` creates a persistent animation. If the component unmounts while recording (e.g., navigation), the loop continues running referencing stale state:

```ts
const recordingAnimRef = useRef<Animated.CompositeAnimation | null>(null);

const startRecording = async () => {
  // ...
  recordingAnimRef.current = Animated.loop(...);
  recordingAnimRef.current.start();
};

// Cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  return () => {
    recordingAnimRef.current?.stop();
  };
}, []);
```

---

**`pulseAnim` loop runs forever** (`CoachScreen.tsx:226`)

The opacity pulse on the empty state icon loops continuously from mount, even when messages exist and the empty state isn't visible. Since `useNativeDriver: true`, it's cheap but wasteful:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (messages.length > 0) return; // skip if not showing empty state
  const anim = Animated.loop(...);
  anim.start();
  return () => anim.stop();
}, [messages.length]); // stop when messages arrive
```

